Web-Based E-Channeling System
SLIIT — SE2030 Software Engineering — Group Project (2026-Y2-S1-MLB-B10G2-04)
A hospital/clinic e-channeling platform built with Spring Boot (MVC) + JSP + Microsoft SQL Server,
matching the use-case diagram, proposal report and activity diagrams already submitted for this project.
---
1. Tech stack
Layer	Technology
Backend	Spring Boot 3.2 (Spring MVC, plain JDBC via `JdbcTemplate` — no JPA/Hibernate)
Database	Microsoft SQL Server
Frontend	JSP + JSTL, plain CSS (no frontend framework)
Auth	Session-based login, BCrypt password hashing (`spring-security-crypto` only — not full Spring Security)
Build	Maven
We used plain JDBC on purpose (instead of Hibernate/JPA) because the DB module (SQL, DDL, stored
procedures, triggers, prepared statements, exception handling) is examinable, and this way every
query in the code is a real SQL statement you can explain in the viva.
---
2. Project structure
```
src/main/java/com/echannel/
 ├─ model/        one class per table (User, Doctor, Patient, Appointment, ...)
 ├─ repository/   Repository<T, ID> generic interface + one impl per entity (raw SQL via JdbcTemplate)
 ├─ service/      business logic, composed of repositories
 ├─ controller/   one controller per actor (Auth, Patient, Doctor, Reception, Pharmacist, Operations, Admin)
 ├─ config/       RoleInterceptor (session-based access control), password + MVC config
 └─ exception/    DatabaseException (custom checked exception)

src/main/webapp/WEB-INF/views/   JSP pages, one folder per role dashboard
src/main/webapp/resources/css/   style.css (modern UI)
db/echannel\\\_schema.sql           run this in SQL Server first
```
This matches the OOP write-up from the brief:
Encapsulation — every model has private fields + public getters/setters.
Abstraction — `Repository<T, ID>` interface hides the JDBC/SQL details from the Service layer.
Inheritance — `DatabaseException extends Exception`.
Polymorphism — every `XxxRepository` overrides the same `Repository<T, ID>` contract; model
classes have overloaded no-arg / all-args constructors.
Composition — Services have-a Repository (constructor/field injection), they don't extend it.
Generics — `Repository<T, ID>` is reused across every entity.
---
3. Database setup (do this first)
Install SQL Server (Express edition is fine) and SQL Server Management Studio (SSMS) or Azure Data Studio.
Open `db/echannel\\\_schema.sql` in SSMS and run the whole script. It will:
create the `EChannelDB` database
create all tables (`users`, `branches`, `rooms`, `doctors`, `doctor\\\_schedule`, `patients`,
`appointments`, `health\\\_records`, `prescriptions`, `feedback`, `notifications`)
create one view (`vw\\\_daily\\\_appointments`), one stored procedure
(`sp\\\_fulfil\\\_prescription`) and one trigger (`trg\\\_prescription\\\_fulfilled`)
insert seed data: 1 branch, 2 rooms, and one demo login per role.
Demo logins (all use password `Passw0rd!`)
Username	Role
admin	System Administrator
drperera	Doctor (Cardiology)
reception1	Reception Staff
pharmacy1	Pharmacist
opsmgr1	Operations Manager
patient1	Patient
---
4. Configure the connection
Edit `src/main/resources/application.properties`:
```properties
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:sqlserver://localhost:1433;databaseName=EChannelDB;encrypt=true;trustServerCertificate=true
spring.datasource.username=sa
spring.datasource.password=YourStrong@Passw0rd
```
Change the username/password to match your own SQL Server login.
---
5. Run the project
```bash
mvn spring-boot:run
```
Then open http://localhost:8080 in your browser. You'll be redirected to `/login`.
> If you'd rather deploy the generated WAR (`target/echannel-system.war`) to an external Tomcat, open
> `pom.xml` and change the `spring-boot-starter-tomcat` dependency scope to `provided`.
---
6. What's implemented vs. the use-case diagram
Actor	Implemented
Patient	Register Account, Login, Search Doctors by Specialty, Book Appointment, Cancel Appointment, View Medical History, View e-Prescriptions, Submit Feedback
Doctor	Login, Manage Availability (create/cancel sessions with conflict checking), View Patient History, Add Diagnosis Notes, Issue e-Prescription
Reception Staff	Login, Register Walk-in Patient, View Daily Appointment List, Update Booking Status (Attended / No-show)
Pharmacist	Login, View e-Prescription Queue, Confirm Order Fulfilment (calls a stored procedure)
Operations Manager	Login, Monitor Room Usage, View Doctor Performance (average rating), View/Flag Feedback
System Administrator	Login, Manage User Roles & Status, Reset Password, Configure Branch Parameters, Add/Deactivate Rooms
Not implemented (kept out of scope to stay within a manageable university project, as allowed by the
proposal's own "Scope and Limitations" section): billing/insurance, telemedicine, SMS/email delivery of
OTPs, and the `<<extend>>` edge cases that are pure UI convenience (e.g. "View Feedback Response").
Feel free to extend these once the core flow is working and understood — they follow the exact same
Model → Repository → Service → Controller → JSP pattern used everywhere else.
---
7. Mapping to the Databases (IT xxxx) module outline
LO1 (DB design / ERD) — see `db/echannel\\\_schema.sql` for the physical schema; draw the
conceptual ERD from these tables and FKs for your Design Document.
LO2 (Schema refinement) — the schema is already in 3NF (e.g. `doctor\\\_schedule` separated from
`appointments`, `rooms` separated from `branches`) — good material to discuss functional
dependencies in your report.
LO3 (SQL) — every `XxxRepository` class is plain SQL via `PreparedStatement`/`JdbcTemplate`;
`FeedbackRepository.averageRatingForDoctor()` uses `AVG()`; `vw\\\_daily\\\_appointments` is a multi-table
join view.
LO4 (DB programming) — `sp\\\_fulfil\\\_prescription` (stored procedure), `trg\\\_prescription\\\_fulfilled`
(trigger), `vw\\\_daily\\\_appointments` (view).
LO5 (DB connectivity) — `PrescriptionRepository.fulfil()` calls the stored procedure via
`SimpleJdbcCall`; every other repository uses `PreparedStatement`-backed `JdbcTemplate` calls wrapped
in try/catch that re-throws a custom `DatabaseException`.
---
8. Notes for your viva / individual contribution section
Because every module (Doctor Schedule, Appointment, Health Records, Branch/Room, Prescription,
Feedback) has its own Model + Repository + Service + a dedicated section of a Controller, it's easy to
divide "ownership" per group member exactly as listed in your proposal report (section 6), and each
member can explain their own file end-to-end (JSP → Controller → Service → Repository → SQL) during
